WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.100
A walking nightmare and a machete wielding&nbsp;
killer clashed 20 years ago in "Freddy vs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.720
Jason," and fans have been begging for a sequel&nbsp;
ever since. Here's why it hasn't happened yet.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.460
The "Nightmare on Elm Street" and "Friday the&nbsp;
13th" franchises were both popular, profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.140
series in the '80s and '90s. Freddy's last film&nbsp;
was almost a decade earlier, and the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.080
Jason flick had been a flop. So when "Freddy&nbsp;
vs. Jason" landed in 2003, combining them into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.820
a crossover film, it re-energized both characters&nbsp;
and wound up pulling in the highest box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.340
gross for both franchises. With an estimated&nbsp;
$116 million dollar haul on a $30 million dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.840
budget, it was a huge money maker, and you'd&nbsp;
think it would have led to a second crossover.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.060
Unfortunately, attempts to get a "Freddy vs.&nbsp;
Jason" sequel have been stalled for years. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.960
wasn't helped by the fact that both franchises&nbsp;
opted to reboot their respective series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.520
with a new "Friday the 13th" arriving&nbsp;
in 2009, and a remake of "Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.360
on Elm Street" a year later. Neither were&nbsp;
successful enough to warrant follow-ups.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
At the same time, the movie rights&nbsp;
to Jason and the "Friday the 13th"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.620
series were soon caught in a complicated&nbsp;
legal web that has never been cleared up.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.880
Attempts to make a sequel to "Freddy vs.&nbsp;
Jason" went further than just pairing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.300
the two villains for a second bout,&nbsp;
however. Looking for a fresh spin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.320
the studio tried adding a third character, and&nbsp;
it seems that they settled on one in particular:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.220
Ash Williams from "Evil Dead." Played&nbsp;
by Bruce Campbell, the chainsaw-wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.120
warrior would have matched up well against&nbsp;
the machete and claws of Jason and Freddy.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.080
The idea actually stems from an Easter&nbsp;
egg in "Jason Goes to Hell," where a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.160
copy of the Necronomicon from "Evil Dead"&nbsp;
is implied to be responsible for Jason's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.660
power. According to a "SyFy" interview with&nbsp;
that film's director, Adam Marcus, he was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.800
quote, "setting up a mythology that Jason's&nbsp;
mom wanted her son back so badly that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.580
reads from the Necronomicon and brings about&nbsp;
the resurrection of her son." As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.420
the notion to cross all three franchises&nbsp;
together seemed like a natural fit.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.260
Unfortunately, getting the rights to&nbsp;
both Freddy and Jason was hard enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.060
but wrangling "Evil Dead" on top of it proved&nbsp;
nearly impossible. Bruce Campbell, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.620
wasn't all that excited about the concept, and&nbsp;
eventually, he walked away. He said on Reddit,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.480
"I'm not real interested in crossovers. One&nbsp;
of the main reasons why 'Ash vs. Jason vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.080
Freddy' did not come to pass is because&nbsp;
we couldn't control any other character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.500
other than Ash. That felt like a&nbsp;
creatively bankrupt way to go."

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.440
"This guy's behind everything."
"Wow, there's an overstatement."

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.240
As the rumors of a three-way battle&nbsp;
with Ash in a sequel gained steam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.340
the film's director, Ronny Yu, addressed the&nbsp;
gossip in 2004. Speaking to "Creature Corner"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.220
about the possibility of merging&nbsp;
the three franchises, he said,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.160
"That's something that the studio&nbsp;
came up with. They asked me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.660
and all the fans wanted me to do it as well,&nbsp;
so I said, 'Yes, show me the script!' I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.900
definitely do it. They're still working&nbsp;
on the script, I haven't seen anything."

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.860
Sadly, Yu's jab at the long development&nbsp;
time was frighteningly prescient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.180
and nothing has yet to come of it some 20 years&nbsp;
later. And he's not the only one who's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.860
frustrated by the lack of movement. The original&nbsp;
film's writers, Damian Shannon and Mark Swift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.300
are still holding out for a sequel to&nbsp;
come to fruition. In 2020, they tweeted,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.620
"We still want to get it made."

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.220
As for the cast, Freddy actor and&nbsp;
star Robert Englund expressed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.480
regret that they didn't do a sequel while&nbsp;
chatting with "Too Fab" in 2020. He said,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.440
"I think that remakes were rushed. I know&nbsp;
the remake on 'A Nightmare on Elm Street'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.640
was premature. We should have done another&nbsp;
'Freddy vs. Jason' film and then waited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.320
they should have waited for five or 10 years."

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.340
As we've already discussed, one of the&nbsp;
reasons a sequel hasn't come yet is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.420
both franchises have since seen clean-slate&nbsp;
reboots. New actors were brought in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.140
with Jackie Earle Haley putting on the&nbsp;
striped sweater to play Freddy and future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.120
"Swamp Thing" actor Derek Mears donning the&nbsp;
hockey mask as Jason. Though the 2009 version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.920
of "Friday the 13th" did okayish at the box&nbsp;
office, neither film got a sequel of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.560
but one of the ideas thrown around by fans is to&nbsp;
essentially do what both franchises did in 2003.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.880
They want to create a crossover mash-up movie&nbsp;
to re-energize both series at the same time.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.680
Recent wild rumors and fan's desires aside,&nbsp;
there's some logic to the notion. Even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.520
it's unlikely that we'd get Ash Williams, a&nbsp;
battle between the rebooted killers could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.900
a winning idea with the right script, a solid&nbsp;
director, and perhaps some legacy actors from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.240
both franchises returning to make a splash.&nbsp;
In fact, during a 2008 conversation with MTV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.800
Derek Mears even said he'd love to do a "Freddy&nbsp;
vs. Jason" sequel, although at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.860
Jackie Earle Haley hadn't been publicly&nbsp;
announced for the "Nightmare" reboot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.340
yet and Mears was saying how he'd love&nbsp;
to throw down against Robert Englund.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.020
"I've been away from my&nbsp;
children for far too long."

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:27.420
Unlike most sequels stuck in development hell, we&nbsp;
know a lot about what the sequel to "Freddy vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.520
Jason" would have been about. That's because in&nbsp;
2004, "Bloody Disgusting" actually published the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.720
screen treatment on their website for all to see.&nbsp;
It was a version that incorporated Ash Williams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.640
and it begins where "Army of Darkness"&nbsp;
and "Freddy vs. Jason" left off. Ash is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.000
searching for the Necronomicon, the town&nbsp;
of Crystal Lake has been redeveloped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
and Jason's house — where the&nbsp;
book resides — is to be torn down.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.020
According to the treatment, Freddy is&nbsp;
"clinging to life" inside Jason's mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.400
and the film opens with Lori Campbell and Will&nbsp;
Rollins returning to Crystal Lake to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.020
both villains are truly dead. After a frightening&nbsp;
quick-kill sequence, we meet our heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.520
a group of teens working at a new S-Mart — the&nbsp;
store where Ash works — built in the new Crystal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.620
Lake. But when Jason learns of the Necronomicon,&nbsp;
he plans to use it to free Freddy from his mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.760
and resurrect his mother. He's not the only one&nbsp;
who knows the book is in Crystal Lake though, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.620
Ash has arrived to find it too, leading him into&nbsp;
a showdown with both Freddy and Jason, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.480
help of his fellow S-Mart employees. Ultimately,&nbsp;
"Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash" would have turned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.440
two villains into reluctant allies while sending&nbsp;
in Bruce Campbell's Ash to stop them both.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.760
Unfortunately, one of the biggest reasons&nbsp;
that a sequel hasn't happened to this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.760
is that actor Robert Englund —&nbsp;
the only man to portray Freddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.180
Krueger until 2010 — is no longer&nbsp;
interested in playing the character.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:45.840
"I won't don the fedora or strap on the claws&nbsp;
again, because I'm just a little too old."

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.320
Englund doubled down on this&nbsp;
in 2023, telling "Variety,"

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.520
"I'm too old and thick to play Freddy&nbsp;
now. I just can't do fight scenes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.360
more than one take anymore. I've got a&nbsp;
bad neck and bad back and arthritis in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.420
my right wrist. So I have to hang&nbsp;
it up, but I would love to cameo."

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:04.680
However, the horror actor did suggest that&nbsp;
Kevin Bacon might make for a good Freddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.080
saying the way he moves might&nbsp;
be perfect for the part.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.740
With all that said, however, if the screen&nbsp;
treatment that had been leaked by "Bloody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.220
Disgusting" had ever been made, we know it&nbsp;
would have starred Englund and Bruce Campbell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.080
as well as Monica Keena and Jason Ritter,&nbsp;
returning as survivors Lori and Will. Keena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.040
even addressed the possibility recently while&nbsp;
talking with the podcast Happy Horror Time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.060
revealing that both she and Ritter had options&nbsp;
for a sequel in their contract for the first film.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:31.500
Of course, it's also likely that Ken Kirzinger&nbsp;
would have reprised his role as Jason too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.580
and he may even still be up for a return&nbsp;
if somehow a "Freddy vs. Jason" follow-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.060
could ever be made. He made this clear&nbsp;
during an appearance at a panel during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.360
the Spookala Horror Convention in 2022, saying,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.940
"If they asked me to do that,&nbsp;
I'd be very happy to come back."

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.780
It's been two decades since "Freddy&nbsp;
vs. Jason" first hit theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.100
but what you might not know is that there is&nbsp;
indeed a continuation of the story — just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:57.900
on film. The crossover sequel took place in&nbsp;
comic book form, a joint production between DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:02.640
Comics imprint Wildstorm and New Jersey-based&nbsp;
independent publisher Dynamite Entertainment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.440
Titled "Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash," the&nbsp;
six-issue miniseries was published in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.660
2007 and written by James Kuhoric from a story&nbsp;
by Jeff Katz, the writer of the planned movie.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.680
Following that original treatment more or&nbsp;
less, the story sees Freddy resurrected by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.880
Jason through the use of the Necronomicon. At&nbsp;
the same time, Ash Williams arrives in Crystal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.440
Lake looking for the cursed book, and an epic&nbsp;
battle ensues, with Freddy resurrecting all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.120
of Jason's previous victims as Deadites that&nbsp;
Ash must slay. But what makes this sequel so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.680
unique is that it even received a sequel of its&nbsp;
own, "Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash: The Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:40.260
Warriors," published in 2009, and once again&nbsp;
written by Kuhoric, based on a story from Katz.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.040
This time around, the U.S. government&nbsp;
tries to control Freddy and Jason's powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.180
only to unleash them once again on an&nbsp;
unsuspecting populace. Then it's up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:52.620
Ash to join forces with former survivors of both&nbsp;
madmen to bring them down and save the world.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.960
Given that "Freddy vs. Jason" brought together two&nbsp;
iconic horror franchises, it makes sense that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.400
amassed a bigger audience than either had achieved&nbsp;
alone. Even after 20 years of frustration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.540
fans are still hoping that they'll finally get&nbsp;
a chance to see Freddy and Jason together again.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:11.580
"The only thing to fear is fear itself."

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.920
Over on Change.org, a petition to get a "Freddy&nbsp;
vs. Jason" sequel collected signatures from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.060
thousands of supporters who were hoping to get&nbsp;
Hollywood's attention. While nothing came of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.980
it did show just how passionate the movie's fans&nbsp;
are. Likewise, over on Reddit, "Freddy vs. Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.060
vs. Ash" often pops up in discussions of&nbsp;
horror sequels fans still want to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.260
and it remains a hot topic of conversation&nbsp;
to this day. Even the press is still in love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:38.460
with articles, podcasts, and websites&nbsp;
continuing to publish discussions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.160
ponder the possibilities of a&nbsp;
part two even decades later.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:46.440
There are many challenges to overcome to make&nbsp;
a "Freddy vs. Jason" sequel happen. A new story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.100
and script will have to be assembled given&nbsp;
Bruce Campbell's disinterest in a crossover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:55.320
and Robert Englund will have to be convinced to&nbsp;
return, or be replaced by a new Freddy. The legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.400
rights to the "Friday the 13th" series will&nbsp;
have to be cleared up, which is no easy feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.720
while the studio will have to be convinced that&nbsp;
such a crossover, 20 years later, can still work.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.840
But even with all that in mind,&nbsp;
there's definitely a way it can happen.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.200
When it comes to the legal problems,&nbsp;
studios have shown that when there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.740
money to be made and a blockbuster to be&nbsp;
produced, there's not much they won't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:17.160
to get the rights they need to make it&nbsp;
happen. For proof, look no further than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.240
Sony's unprecedented deal with Marvel&nbsp;
to incorporate Spider-Man into the MCU.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.900
We admit, it's probably a longshot with&nbsp;
everything that's working against it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.760
but it's what the fans clearly crave, and with&nbsp;
crossovers and cinematic universes all the rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.040
it could stand to make big box office bucks.

